## 12. Are the backtest and accuracy figures reproducible?

**No. Not from this repository, and not from the live public endpoint.**

### 12.1 What the live API returns today

`GET https://neuralquant.onrender.com/testing/quarterly/public-results` returned exactly `_q1fy27_baseline()`:

- quarter Q1FY27, run_date 2026-06-02
- summary: alpha **13.65**, hit rate **88.1**, average return **7.27**, benchmark **−6.38**, 42 selections
- pools: LM250 alpha **12.69** / hit **90.9**; SmallCap **14.83** / **87.5**; Turnaround **13.37** / **86.7**

Those three pool names and the summary shape are produced only by the hardcoded function. The database path returns a single pool named from `test_type`. So production is serving the fallback. That matches the bundled CSV: `quarterly_test_results.csv` has **42 rows, 0 filled `return_pct`, 0 filled `exit_price`**.

### 12.2 The hardcoded list does not match the selected stocks

`quarterly_test_runs.csv` (3 runs, all 2026-06-02) selected, among others, YESBANK, HEROMOTOCO, JSWSTEEL, BAJAJ-AUTO, TATASTEEL, RBLBANK, PERSISTENT, ADANIPORTS, GRASIM, BOSCHLTD, NYKAA, IOC, FEDERALBNK, OFSS, MANAPPURAM, HINDALCO, PFC.

`_q1fy27_baseline()`’s LM250 list is ADANIPORTS, PERSISTENT, GRASIM, TITAN, TCS, INFY, HCLTECH, WIPRO, COFORGE, LT, HEROMOTOCO. Overlap is partial. TCS, INFY, WIPRO, TITAN, COFORGE, LT are **not** in the selection snapshot. YESBANK, JSWSTEEL, BAJAJ-AUTO, TATASTEEL are **not** in the return list.

The smallcap and turnaround lists inside the same function repeat the same return series under fake tickers (`ADANIPORTS2`, `TCS2`, `TCS3`, `COFORGE2`, …). Recomputing from those lists:

| Pool | Count | Average return from the list | Alpha vs −6.38 | Hit rate (return &gt; −6.38) | Hardcoded `pool_breakdown` |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| LM250 list | 11 | 6.47 | 12.85 | 90.9 | avg 6.31, alpha **12.69** |
| Smallcap list | 16 | 8.00 | 14.38 | 87.5 | avg **8.45**, alpha **14.83** |
| Turnaround list | 15 | 7.08 | 13.46 | 86.7 | avg **6.99**, alpha **13.37** |
| All 42 (with duplicates) | 42 | **7.27** | **13.65** | **88.1** | summary uses these computed figures |

The summary block is arithmetic on the (duplicated) lists. The pool block is a second, inconsistent set of constants. Hit rates happen to match; averages do not. Doubling and tripling the same winners is what produces “42 selections”.

### 12.3 The methodology page is a third set of numbers

`apps/web/src/app/methodology/page.tsx` hardcodes Alpha **13.5%**, Hit rate **89%**, Average return **24.8%**, Nifty 50 **+11.3%**, max drawdown **−8.2% vs −12.1%**, and the sentence “IRS% &gt; 65” and “No cherry-picking.” The live API’s benchmark is **−6.38%**, not +11.3%, and its average return is **7.27%**, not 24.8%. A +13.5 alpha versus a +11.3 benchmark implies a ~24.8 portfolio return (11.3+13.5). A +13.65 alpha versus a −6.38 benchmark implies a ~7.3 portfolio return. Both stories use “about 13.5 alpha” and “about 89% hit rate” and they cannot both be the same experiment. Layout metadata on the same page says “87%+”.

The README’s “+13.5% avg alpha vs Nifty50, ~89% hit rate” follows the methodology page. The prospectus’s “+12.69% to +14.83%, hit rate 87–91%” follows the hardcoded pool constants. `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` says “+12.7% to +14.8% alpha, 87–91% hit rate”.

### 12.4 FinDebate +20.4% and “90% accuracy”

FinDebate +20.4% alpha is one sentence in `README.md` line 84, offered as external academic support. Nothing in the repo downloads, cites, or recomputes it. The “90%+” line is marketing (LinkedIn, May 2026), not an output of `run_walk_forward`.

### 12.5 What *would* make it reproducible

The quarterly harness (`POST /testing/quarterly/evaluate`) is capable of filling `exit_price` from yfinance and computing alpha against Nifty. It has not been run to completion on the stored entries: every exit in the snapshot is blank, and production still returns the fallback. Until someone evaluates those entry rows — or replaces the page with the walk-forward endpoint fed by real `score_cache_history` — the published track record is a static JSON object.

