### 2.2 Patentability and ownership notes [INFERENCE unless cited]
- **Section 3(k) hurdle.** The 2025 CRI Guidelines treat s3(k) as four separate exclusions: mathematical methods, **business methods**, computer programmes per se, and algorithms. Software or algorithm claims can pass if, taken as a whole, they show a technical effect or technical contribution (https://ipindia.gov.in/frontend/pdf/patents/guidelines/GUIDELINES%20FOR%20EXAMINATION%20OF%20COMPUTER%20RELATED%20INVENTIONS%20%28CRIs%29%20-%202025.pdf ; summary at https://treelife.in/legal/patenting-software-ai-and-algorithms-in-india/).
  - "Generating equity research signals" reads like a business or financial method.
  - The **verification gate** (deterministic interception and correction of LLM numeric outputs against provenance-tagged data, with an audit log) is the more plausible "technical effect" framing. That is consistent with Satyam saying the patent "leads on" it.
- **Ownership.** The applicant is Satyam personally. The README lists "scoring methodology IP" in a sale package and says "Patent strategy: IP_PROTECTION/MEETING_PREP.md". The QuantAlpha AI JV (May 2026) and his employment at Financial Simplicity could each affect who owns or gets licences to the IP. Public sources say nothing on this. Check JV and employment agreements before the complete spec or any sale.
- **Prior art.** TradingAgents (Dec 2024, bull/bear debate plus risk team), FinDebate (Sep 2025, Skeptic agent), AlphaAgents (Aug 2025, debate to consensus) and FinRobot are all public (see §3.1), so claims to "adversarial debate" alone will meet close prior art. That matches Satyam's own "2/5" strength note.
- Bryan Leonard's public comment on the patent post raises useful points for drafting the complete spec: fail-open versus fail-closed when no authoritative data exists, "stale-but-authoritative" data (time-aware tolerances), and an abstain path for the adversary (same LinkedIn URL).

