## 4. Regulation

### 4.1 Australia (ASIC, Corporations Act 2001)

- **What counts as advice.** "Financial product advice is a recommendation or statement of opinion which is intended to influence, or which could reasonably be regarded as being intended to influence, a person making a decision in relation to financial products." Factual information is not advice, but presenting facts in a way that "conveys a recommendation … could breach the law" (ASIC INFO 269: https://www.asic.gov.au/regulatory-resources/financial-services/giving-financial-product-advice/discussing-financial-products-and-services-online).
- **Licensing.** "Carrying on a financial services business without an AFS licence is an offence … unless you are authorised as a representative of an AFS licensee or an exemption applies"; penalties run up to 5 years' imprisonment for individuals and millions of dollars for corporations (INFO 269, same URL). **General advice is still regulated and needs a licence** (same).
- **General versus personal advice (RG 244).**
  - Personal advice is advice where you considered, or a reasonable person might expect you to have considered, the client's objectives, financial situation or needs (s766B(3)) (RG 244.35).
  - General advice needs a **general advice warning** (s949A(2)) (RG 244.38–39).
  - Using a client's personal information to tailor advice makes it personal advice, and "You cannot avoid this by giving a general advice warning" (RG 244.47).
  - RG 244: https://asic.gov.au/regulatory-resources/find-a-document/regulatory-guides/rg-244-giving-information-general-advice-and-scaled-advice/ ; PDF: https://download.asic.gov.au/media/tkqi11il/rg244-published-13-december-2012-20211208.pdf (raw/asic_rg244.pdf)
- **Digital / robo-advice (RG 255).** An algorithmic advice provider needs an AFSL or authorised-representative status. Other requirements:
  - at least one responsible manager meeting adviser competence standards
  - people who understand the algorithm
  - documented design, testing and change control, with records kept for 7 years
  - monitoring, and the ability to suspend advice if there is a defect
  - Sources: https://www.asic.gov.au/regulatory-resources/find-a-document/regulatory-guides/rg-255-providing-digital-financial-product-advice-to-retail-clients ; https://download.asic.gov.au/media/vbnlotqw/rg255-published-30-august-2016-20220328.pdf
- **AI governance.** REP 798 "Beware the gap" (Oct 2024) creates no new rules. It says existing technology-neutral obligations apply to AI and expects AI inventories, named accountability, human oversight, monitoring and vendor-model governance (https://www.asic.gov.au/regulatory-resources/find-a-document/reports/rep-798-beware-the-gap-governance-arrangements-in-the-face-of-ai-innovation). ASIC's Feb 2026 financial advice update likewise added **no AI-specific advice rules** [from search summary] (https://www.asic.gov.au/about-asic/news-centre/news-items/financial-advice-update-february-2026).
- **Misleading conduct** applies even without a licence: "If you make a prediction, such as a future return … you must have reasonable grounds" (INFO 269). Related guides: RG 36 (licensing), RG 234 (advertising), RG 274 (design and distribution obligations), all listed in INFO 269.

**What an Australian startup would need [INFERENCE, based on the sources above]:**
1. **Either stay factual** (data, scores without buy/sell language, education), **or** get licensed. The quickest route is becoming a **Corporate Authorised Representative of an existing AFSL holder**, which is Simply Wall St's model (AR 467183 under AFSL 337927).
2. Limit the service to **general advice**, with a prominent s949A warning and no tailoring to a user's "risk profile and goals". Tailoring makes it personal advice, which brings the best-interests duty, SOAs and much higher cost.
3. Follow RG 255-style algorithm governance: documentation, testing, change logs, human oversight and a kill switch.
4. Make no unsubstantiated accuracy or return claims ("90% accuracy", "Proven Edge"), and present backtests conservatively.
5. Have dispute resolution (AFCA membership through the licensee) and compensation arrangements. These are standard AFSL obligations; check the details with the licensee.

### 4.2 India (SEBI)

- **Research Analyst (RA) regulations apply whatever the methodology.** "Providing research services (including buy/sell/hold recommendation) on a security or securities based on any methodology (including the technical analysis) is not exempted from the purview of the RA Regulations." "No person shall act as a research analyst … or hold itself out as a research analyst unless he has obtained a certificate of registration from SEBI" (SEBI RA FAQs, July 2025: https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/faqfiles/jul-2025/1753269723942.pdf ; raw/sebi_1753269723942.txt).
- **Algorithmic or black-box tools.** In SEBI's retail algo-trading framework, providers of "black box" algos must "Register as a Research Analyst and maintain a detailed research report for each such algo" (https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/feb-2025/1738665456458.pdf).
- **Use of AI.** Under the RA Third Amendment Regulations (16 Dec 2024) and the RA guidelines circular (8 Jan 2025), RAs using AI tools must disclose the extent of AI use and remain "solely responsible" for data security, AI-output research and compliance. Existing clients had to be told by 30 Apr 2025. Investment Advisers (IAs) have parallel duties. Sources:
  - https://www.sebi.gov.in/legal/regulations/dec-2024/securities-and-exchange-board-of-india-research-analysts-third-amendment-regulations-2024_89979.html
  - https://www.sebi.gov.in/legal/circulars/jan-2025/guidelines-for-research-analysts_90634.html
  - IA guidelines: https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/jan-2025/1736338642778.pdf
  - Consolidated in the **Feb 2026 Master Circular for RAs**: https://www.sebi.gov.in/legal/master-circulars/feb-2026/master-circular-for-research-analysts_99571.html
- **Fee cap.** RA fees for individual and HUF clients are capped at ₹1,51,000 per family per year (Jan 2025 RA guidelines; same URLs).
- **Registration requirements** (RA FAQs and NISM):
  - application through RAASB (BSE)
  - application fee ₹2,000 and registration fee ₹3,000 (individuals and partnerships)
  - deposit of ₹1 lakh for up to 150 clients, rising with client count, and no capital-adequacy requirement
  - NISM Series XV certification and qualifying degree
  - Sources: https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/faqfiles/jul-2025/1753269723942.pdf ; https://www.nism.ac.in/blog/prerequisites-for-becoming-a-sebi-registered-research-analyst
- **Finfluencers.** SEBI's Jan 2025 circular limits "education" content to price data at least 3 months old, bans unregistered advice and return/performance claims dressed up as education, and bars SEBI-regulated entities from associating with unregistered persons doing those things (https://www.business-standard.com/markets/news/sebi-finfluencer-circular-live-stock-data-market-education-rules-125013000571_1.html).
- **Enforcement example.** In April 2026 SEBI acted against paid Telegram channels it found to be unregistered RAs and IAs (https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/attachdocs/apr-2026/ORDER_1777529838.pdf) [from search summary].
- **Draft AI/ML principles.** SEBI's June 2025 consultation proposed model governance, disclosure of AI use, testing, 5-year retention of inputs and outputs, bias controls and audits (https://www.sebi.gov.in/reports-and-statistics/reports/jun-2025/consultation-paper-on-guidelines-for-responsible-usage-of-ai-ml-in-indian-securities-markets_94687.html). **Uncertain:** I could not confirm whether these were finalised in 2026.
- **Investment Adviser vs RA [INFERENCE].** Personalised allocation by risk profile is investment advice and falls under the IA Regulations, a heavier regime. Impersonal stock-level buy/sell/hold calls fall under the RA Regulations.

**What an Indian-facing startup would need [INFERENCE]:**
1. If it issues buy/sell/hold verdicts or "Strong Buy" bands on named Indian securities for paying users, **register as an RA** (individual or company), or partner with a registered RA who takes responsibility for the published research.
2. Include RA disclosures, AI-use disclosure, client KYC and consent, record keeping and fee-cap compliance.
3. Do not offer personalised allocation unless registered as an IA.
4. Make no performance or return claims in marketing unless they comply.
5. A disclaimer saying "not SEBI-registered" **does not** take buy/sell/hold research outside the rules. SEBI's test looks at the activity.

### 4.3 United States (SEC / FINRA)

- **Investment Advisers Act and the publisher's exclusion.** Under *Lowe v. SEC*, 472 U.S. 181 (1985), a bona fide publication offering impersonal, disinterested commentary, generally available and regularly circulated, is excluded from the "investment adviser" definition. Personalised advice or touting is not (https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/472/181). [INFERENCE] Advice tailored to a user's risk profile, or individualised portfolio construction, undercuts the exclusion. Then RIA registration is needed: state-level below the SEC thresholds, or SEC. Magnifi chose to register as an RIA (https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/crs/crs_309397.pdf).
- **Marketing Rule.** Hypothetical or backtested performance in RIA advertising requires policies ensuring relevance to the audience, plus disclosures. It is generally hard to justify in mass-market retail ads (https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/small-business-compliance-guides/investment-adviser-marketing ; https://www.troutman.com/insights/the-secs-new-marketing-rule-practically-speaking-hypothetical-performance/).
- **"AI-washing" enforcement.** On 18 Mar 2024 the SEC settled with Delphia ($225k) and Global Predictions ($175k) over false or misleading AI claims. Global Predictions had claimed to be the "first regulated AI financial advisor" and to offer "Expert AI-driven forecasts" (https://www.sec.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2024-36).
- **SEC FY2026 exam priorities** include whether AI claims are "fair, accurate, and substantiated" and whether algorithmic recommendations fit client profiles (https://www.sec.gov/files/2026-exam-priorities.pdf) [from search summary].
- **The predictive-data-analytics rule was withdrawn** on 12 June 2025, so there is no AI-specific conflicts rule pending (https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/06/17/2025-11110/withdrawal-of-proposed-regulatory-actions).
- **FINRA** (relevant only if partnering with or becoming a broker-dealer): Regulatory Notice 24-09 says existing rules apply to GenAI, including Rule 3110 supervision and Rule 2210 communications (https://www.finra.org/rules-guidance/notices/24-09). FINRA Rule 2241 covers research analysts at member firms.

**What a US-facing startup would need [INFERENCE]:** keep the product an impersonal publication with the same content for every subscriber and no tailoring. Avoid backtested-performance marketing and unsubstantiated "accuracy" claims. Add clear disclaimers. If the product moves into personalised recommendations or portfolio construction, register as an RIA (state or SEC) and comply with the Marketing Rule, Form ADV/CRS and fiduciary duties.

