### Direction A: NeuralQuant Verify (a verification and audit layer for licensed research publishers)

- **Target customer.** Small licensed publishers who already use LLMs to draft research:
  - **India:** SEBI-registered Research Analysts, which includes small firms and individual RAs publishing stock notes.
  - **US:** newsletter publishers relying on the *Lowe* publisher exclusion, and small RIAs.
  - **Australia, later:** AFSL holders and Corporate Authorised Representatives publishing general-advice research. These come only after the employer check in §1.4.

  **[ASSUMPTION]** that enough of these publishers already use LLMs to make a market. The first four weeks test exactly this.
- **Problem.** SEBI's December 2024 amendment and January 2025 guidelines say RAs using AI must disclose that use and remain "solely responsible" for the AI-generated research (https://www.sebi.gov.in/legal/circulars/jan-2025/guidelines-for-research-analysts_90634.html) [EXT §4.2]. SEBI's June 2025 draft AI/ML principles propose testing, audits and 5-year retention of inputs and outputs. Whether they were finalised is unconfirmed (https://www.sebi.gov.in/reports-and-statistics/reports/jun-2025/consultation-paper-on-guidelines-for-responsible-usage-of-ai-ml-in-indian-securities-markets_94687.html) [EXT §4.2]. ASIC expects AI governance, human oversight and monitoring (REP 798: https://www.asic.gov.au/regulatory-resources/find-a-document/reports/rep-798-beware-the-gap-governance-arrangements-in-the-face-of-ai-innovation) and requires "reasonable grounds" for predictions (INFO 269) [EXT §4.1]. The SEC has fined firms for AI-washing (https://www.sec.gov/newsroom/press-releases/2024-36) and examines whether AI claims are substantiated [EXT §4.3]. LLMs get numbers wrong, as your own production cases show [EXT §5.3]. Today a licensee's options are to check every number by hand or to publish errors under their own registration.
- **Offer.** The publisher pastes or uploads a draft, or calls the API. They get back:
  - every numeric claim, extracted
  - each claim checked against a dated, source-tagged reference value
  - a status for each claim: verified, corrected (with the proposed value), estimated or unavailable
  - a one-page verification certificate per note
  - an append-only audit log they can export to their compliance file
  - an AI-use disclosure paragraph they can paste into the note

  Verify never rates a stock.
- **Pricing.** There is **no direct comparable** in the research for AI-output verification tools, and that is a gap to fill during interviews.
  - Anchors: retail research tools sit at $16–$134/month (Kavout, Danelfin; table above). Danelfin's API tier is $134/month. Enterprise research is quote-based (AlphaSense).
  - **[ASSUMPTION] proposed starting price:** Solo $49/month (about ₹3,999) for up to 100 notes; Firm $199/month for multiple seats, API access and an audit export; usage overage at about $0.25 per note. The $0.25 mirrors your Apify full-AI price, which at least confirms what one LLM pass costs you.
  - India price sensitivity is real: RA income from individuals is capped at ₹1,51,000 per family per year (table above).
- **Go-to-market.**
  - Direct outreach to RAs listed on SEBI's public register of intermediaries, and to US newsletter writers who openly say they use AI.
  - Free "verification report" on one of their published notes, as the opener.
  - RA compliance consultants in India as a referral channel [ASSUMPTION: such consultants exist and will refer; check during interviews].
  - LinkedIn posts showing real catches (anonymised), since evidence suits your style.
  - Later: an open-source checker library as a funnel (Direction G).
- **Reuses.**
  - The two validator functions as the starting point [CODE §4.3].
  - The FMP, EDGAR, FRED and NSE bhavcopy connectors as reference data [CODE §4.5].
  - Supabase and auth [CODE §4.8].
  - The `security_events`-style audit table pattern [CODE §4.8].
  - Stripe checkout [CODE §4.8].
  - The FastAPI app shell.

  About 40% of the needed code exists in some form [ASSUMPTION: rough estimate].
- **Must build.**
  - A general claim extractor covering any numeric field, units, periods and currencies, using an LLM with structured output plus deterministic parsing.
  - A provenance-tagged reference store with as-of timestamps.
  - Tolerance rules that account for data age.
  - Persisted correction and reliability states.
  - The audit log and its export.
  - A simple upload UI.
  - An API key system and a usage meter.
  - An evaluation harness that measures the gate's precision and recall.
- **Regulatory path.** **[INFERENCE]** Checking a licensee's numbers is a software service to the licensee, not financial product advice or research to the public, because the licensee stays the publisher and the responsible party. Confirm this with counsel in each country, especially the Australian question of whether any part of it could be "advice". You also need to:
  - avoid any marketing that says "compliance guaranteed"
  - keep client drafts confidential (data-processing terms, retention controls)
  - check that your reference-data licence allows showing values to paying users. FMP's terms are unknown [CODE §4.5]. The safer design lets customers bring their own data source.
- **Time to first revenue.** **[ASSUMPTION]** 10–14 weeks: four weeks of interviews, four weeks of MVP, then pilots converting.
- **Revenue range, first 12 months [ASSUMPTION].**
  - Low: 2 Solo customers × $49 × 12 = **about $1.2k**
  - Mid: 8 customers averaging $120/month = **about $11.5k ARR**
  - High: 20 customers averaging $199/month = **about $48k ARR**

  These are illustrations, not forecasts. The real input is how many of the first 20 interviews turn into pilots.
- **Key risks.**
  - Publishers may not use LLMs heavily yet, or may not see number errors as their problem.
  - Price sensitivity in India.
  - Frontier models and AlphaSense-type tools adding grounding and citations themselves.
  - Overlap with your employer's field (§1.4).
  - Reference-data licensing.
  - Gate false corrections. Correcting a right number to a wrong one would be worse than catching nothing.
- **Kill criterion.** By day 30, fewer than 3 of 15 or more licensed publishers agree to pilot on real documents. Or by day 90, no paid customer and no signed LOI. Or the gate's false-correction rate on your labelled set stays above 2% [ASSUMPTION: threshold].

